  "problem": "Let\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].$\nWhat are the dimensions of $CD?$",
  "worked_solution": "We notice that $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $D$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $C$ equals the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $CD$ has dimensions $3\\times 2:$ \n\n$$\n{C}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{D}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={CD}_{3\\times 2}\n$$",
